Dear Members of Parliament,

I attach the open correspondence I sent to the Prime Minister and to Sir Ma=
rk Rowley, Commissioner of the Metropolitan Police, on 21 September 2026, t=
ogether with the official transcripts of the final hearing in my financial =
remedy proceedings. I obtained those transcripts this month, more than two =
years after the judgment was published.

Paragraph 64 of the judgment found that I had "become obsessed" with hidden=
 assets. The judgment went on to describe my legal spending as reckless "in=
 the context of the wife's obsessive approach", and added =A3800,000 back a=
gainst my share. A costs order of =A3255,654.50 followed, relying on the sa=
me authority.

The transcripts now allow the judgment to be tested against the record of w=
hat was said in court.

They record that on the fourth day the judge stopped the hearing because "M=
rs Zahmoul is plainly in distress", and that my psychiatrist's letter was b=
efore him from the first day. Neither appears in the judgment.

For the first time since April 2024, I can set my own memory of that hearin=
g against an objective record. The dissonance between what I remembered and=
 what the judgment recorded had caused me severe distress. I could not make=
 the two accounts fit. Two plus two is not five. I now understand why.

What happened in that courtroom no longer depends on my recollection. It ca=
n be read, tested and independently verified.

I am not in a position to pay the costs ordered. I have received no distrib=
ution under the financial order, and the settlement it provides falls well =
below what was anticipated.

Other matters remain unresolved. Two digital devices were placed into court=
-linked custody in April 2021. A review of them was required and never carr=
ied out, and their whereabouts are unknown. Criminal referrals arising from=
 that have been made and are not yet determined.

I have commissioned an independent forensic reconstruction of the evidentia=
l record, to be followed by an independent legal assessment. I am not askin=
g Parliament to determine disputed facts or to interfere with a judicial de=
cision. I am making the underlying evidence independently verifiable, becau=
se the processes through which I sought examination of these matters are ex=
hausted without one having examined the primary record.

From now on, the evidence, the reconstruction, the assessments and the inst=
itutional responses to them will form an external public record.

I want to be clear about why. I have spent six years using the processes av=
ailable to me. The cost has been severe: financial hardship, repeated setba=
cks and periods of despair. I say it once, because it is part of the record=
 too.

What remains is the evidence. It can now be examined.

Yours sincerely,

Nadia Zahmoul
